From: Anaconda Recipes
Subject: [PATCH] Handle MissingCUDAException in fp_quantizer.is_compatible

op_builder/fp_quantizer.py:37 calls installed_cuda_version() unconditionally
inside the `torch.cuda.is_available()` branch. installed_cuda_version() raises
MissingCUDAException when CUDA_HOME is None — which happens on any GPU machine
that has the CUDA runtime libs (so torch.cuda.is_available() returns True) but
no CUDA toolkit / nvcc installed. This breaks `import deepspeed` at import
time on such systems.

Catch the exception and mark the op incompatible — same effect as the existing
cuda_okay = False branches further down.

--- a/op_builder/fp_quantizer.py
+++ b/op_builder/fp_quantizer.py
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 except ImportError:
     pkg_version = None

-from .builder import CUDAOpBuilder, installed_cuda_version
+from .builder import CUDAOpBuilder, MissingCUDAException, installed_cuda_version


 class FPQuantizerBuilder(CUDAOpBuilder):
@@ -34,7 +34,10 @@
         cuda_okay = True
         if not os.environ.get("DS_IGNORE_CUDA_DETECTION"):
             if not self.is_rocm_pytorch() and torch.cuda.is_available():  #ignore-cuda
-                sys_cuda_major, _ = installed_cuda_version()
+                try:
+                    sys_cuda_major, _ = installed_cuda_version()
+                except MissingCUDAException:
+                    return False
                 torch_cuda_major = int(torch.version.cuda.split('.')[0])
                 cuda_capability = torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).major  #ignore-cuda
                 if cuda_capability < 8:
